  "body": "Part of #4881.\n\nThe proof linked from `erdos_427` is not complete on its own. It declares Shiu's theorem as an axiom and derives the result from it:\n\n```lean\naxiom shiu_consecutive_primes\n    (l : ℕ) (hl : 1 ≤ l) (a q : ℕ) (hq : 1 ≤ q) (haq : Nat.Coprime a q) (N : ℕ) :\n    ∃ m, N ≤ m ∧ ∀ i, i < l → nthPrime (m + i) ≡ a [MOD q]\n```\n\nThe attribute said `formal_proof`, which claims a complete proof. It now says `conditional formal_proof ... assuming erdos_427.variants.shiu`, which is the mechanism #4368 added. This is the first use of it in the repository.\n\nThe file already stated the assumption, as `erdos_427.variants.shiu : ShiuTheorem`. The two forms agree: the gist writes `nthPrime` for `Nat.nth Nat.Prime`, and \"for every `N` there is an `m ≥ N`\" is the same as the infinite set of starting indices that `ShiuTheorem` uses.\n\n### Why the declarations moved\n\nThe `assuming` clause resolves the name when the attribute elaborates, so the assumption must come first. `ShiuTheorem` and `erdos_427.variants.shiu` now sit above `erdos_427`. Nothing else about them changed. A comment in the docstring records why they are in that order.\n\n### Checks I ran\n\n- `lake build FormalConjectures.ErdosProblems.«427»` completes with no error and no warning. Before the move it failed with `Unknown constant erdos_427.variants.shiu`, which is what made the order necessary.\n- `extract_names` fills `proofConditions` from the attribute and prints it when it is not empty (`scripts/extract_names.lean:312`). So this makes `erdos_427` the first entry in the published extract to carry that field. I read the code rather than run the extract, which needs the whole library built.\n\n### Not in this change\n\n- 750 and 1141 have the same defect, but neither states its assumption yet. 750 assumes Stiebitz's theorem about the generalised Mycielskian, which Mathlib does not have at all, so the statement has to be built first. 1141 assumes Pollack's Theorem 1.3 and Mertens' third theorem.\n- `check_erdos_status.py` still counts a conditional proof as formally solved. The fix belongs on #4828, which replaces the regex scanner with the published extract, and can therefore read `proofConditions` directly. Patching the regex here would add code that #4828 deletes.\n",
